<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcomannic Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">166–<a href="/years/180">180</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marcomannic Wars were a series of conflicts fought from about 166 to 180 CE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and Germanic and Sarmatian peoples of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, including the Marcomanni, Quadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a>. They began with invasions that reached northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> spent much of his reign on the frontier, and peace was concluded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars.jpg" alt="Marcomannic Wars" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nico Kokkonen · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Column_of_Marcus_Aurelius_-_detail4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> of 161 to 166, fought under the joint rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a>, ended successfully, but the returning troops brought the Antonine Plague, which killed an estimated 7 to 8 million people. At the same time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> began to move south-east from the mouth of the River Vistula, putting pressure on the Germanic tribes to the north and east, and raids crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Until then the Marcomanni and Quadi had generally enjoyed friendly relations and trade with the Empire. The Marcomanni sought incorporation into the Empire on favourable economic terms, which Marcus was unwilling to accept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In late 166 thousands of Langobardi and Lacringi crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and were quickly defeated, and the governor Marcus Iallius Bassus negotiated a truce with eleven tribes. The plague delayed a punitive expedition until 168, when Marcus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a> set up headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>; Verus died of a stroke in January 169. In autumn 169 Marcus resumed the campaign, selling imperial property to pay for it. The Marcomanni under King Ballomar then defeated a Roman force of 20,000 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a>, destroyed Oderzo and besieged Aquileia, the first hostile incursion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> in 101 BCE.</p><p>Command passed to Tiberius Claudius Pompeianus, with the future emperor Pertinax under him, and by late 171 the invaders had been expelled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> signed treaties with the Quadi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a> and in 172 crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and subdued the Marcomanni, and Marcus took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>.</p><p>In 173 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-xii-fulminata">Legio XII Fulminata</a>, surrounded in a campaign against the Quadi, was saved by a storm, the Miracle of the Rain: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> credited an Egyptian magician invoking Mercury, and the Christian writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a> credited Christian prayer. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a> surrendered in 175, and their king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanticus">Zanticus</a>, returned 100,000 Roman prisoners. The revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avidius-cassius">Avidius Cassius</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> interrupted plans for new provinces named Marcomannia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatia">Sarmatia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Quadi rebelled in 177 and the Marcomanni joined them, and Marcus reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a> in August 178 for a second campaign. He died at Vindobona on 17 March 180. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a> ignored his generals' advice, negotiated peace with the Marcomanni and Quadi and held a triumph on 22 October 180.</p><p>The wars showed the weakness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s northern defences, and afterwards 16 of the Empire's 33 legions were stationed along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Germanic settlement in frontier regions grew; settlers at Ravenna rebelled and briefly seized the city, and Marcus banned further settlement of barbarians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The wars are said to have foreshadowed the invasions of the fourth and fifth centuries, and Marcus began writing his Meditations during these campaigns.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcomannic Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Marcomannic Wars</b> some Marcomanni were settled in Italy and other parts of the empire, Gallienus settled others in Pannonia Superior, and by the late 4th century at least some had become Christians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Iazyges, who settled between the Danube and the Tisza, destroyed a Roman legion in Domitian's Sarmatian war of 92 CE; the Roxolani joined Decebalus of Dacia against Trajan; and in the <b>Marcomannic Wars</b> the Iazyges fought Marcus Aurelius until their king Zanticus made peace in 175 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Marcomannic Wars</b>, in about 170 CE, they broke through the Danube frontier, swept through Moesia, Thrace and Macedonia, and plundered the mysteries sanctuary at Eleusis near Athens.</blockquote><a href="/state/costoboci">Costoboci</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hermunduri took part in the <b>Marcomannic Wars</b> against Rome in the 160s and 170s.</blockquote><a href="/state/hermunduri">Hermunduri</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Marcomannic Wars</b> they invaded Pannonia; after defeat on the frozen Danube, King Zanticus made peace with Marcus Aurelius in 175 CE and sent 8,000 cavalry, many of them posted to Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/iazyges">Iazyges</a></li><li><blockquote>They joined the <b>Marcomannic Wars</b> from 166; the 'Rain Miracle' depicted on the Column of Marcus Aurelius took place on their territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/quadi">Quadi</a></li></ul></section>
